AbbreviationIsSubset. $A \subseteq B$, if and only if $A \subseteq B$.

AbbreviationIsSubset. $A$ is a subset of $B$, if and only if $A \subseteq B$.

AbbreviationIsSubset. $A \subseteq B$, if and only if $A$ is a subset of $B$.

AbbreviationIsSubset. $A$ is a subset of $B$, if and only if $A$ is a subset of $B$.

AbbreviationSupseteq. $B \supseteq A$, if and only if $A \subseteq B$.

AbbreviationSupseteq. $B \supseteq A$, if and only if $A$ is a subset of $B$.

AbbreviationSupseteq. $B$ is a superset of $A$, if and only if $A \subseteq B$.

AbbreviationSupseteq. $B$ is a superset of $A$, if and only if $A$ is a subset of $B$.

PropositionSubseteqRefl. $A \subseteq A$.

PropositionSubseteqRefl. $A$ is a subset of $A$.

PropositionSubseteqAntisymmetric. Assume that $A \subseteq B \subseteq A$. Then $A = B$.

PropositionSubseteqAntisymmetric. Assume that $A \subseteq B \subseteq A$. Then $A$ is equal to $B$.

PropositionSubseteqAntisymmetric. Assume that $A$ is a subset of $B$ and $B \subseteq A$. Then $A = B$.

PropositionSubseteqAntisymmetric. Assume that $A \subseteq B$ and $B$ is a subset of $A$. Then $A = B$.

PropositionSubseteqAntisymmetric. Assume that $A$ is a subset of $B$ and $B$ is a subset of $A$. Then $A = B$.

PropositionSubseteqAntisymmetric. Assume that $A$ is a subset of $B$ and $B \subseteq A$. Then $A$ is equal to $B$.

PropositionSubseteqAntisymmetric. Assume that $A \subseteq B$ and $B$ is a subset of $A$. Then $A$ is equal to $B$.

PropositionSubseteqAntisymmetric. Assume that $A$ is a subset of $B$ and $B$ is a subset of $A$. Then $A$ is equal to $B$.

PropositionElemSubseteq. Assume that $a \in A \subseteq B$. Then $a \in B$.

PropositionElemSubseteq. Assume that $a \in A \subseteq B$. Then $a$ is an element of $B$.

PropositionElemSubseteq. Assume that $a \in A$ and $A$ is a subset of $B$. Then $a \in B$.

PropositionElemSubseteq. Assume that $a$ is an element of $A$ and $A \subseteq B$. Then $a \in B$.

PropositionElemSubseteq. Assume that $a$ is an element of $A$ and $A$ is a subset of $B$. Then $a \in B$.

PropositionElemSubseteq. Assume that $a \in A$ and $A$ is a subset of $B$. Then $a$ is an element of $B$.

PropositionElemSubseteq. Assume that $a$ is an element of $A$ and $A \subseteq B$. Then $a$ is an element of $B$.

PropositionElemSubseteq. Assume that $a$ is an element of $A$ and $A$ is a subset of $B$. Then $a$ is an element of $B$.

